A = {
"eu14400": dict(
 bluf="Printing turned copying from a slow craft into an industry: a text could now be reproduced by the thousand and, once out, could not be recalled. That collapse in the cost of spreading ideas made the Reformation, the Scientific Revolution and standardized national languages possible.",
 why="Europe already had the ingredients: paper (spreading since the 1300s), a growing urban market of merchants, lawyers, clergy and students, and skilled metalworkers. Gutenberg, trained as a goldsmith, combined them into a working system. The demand existed before the machine.",
 then="Printed texts were identical, so scholars could compare, correct and build on one another's work. Pamphlets let dissidents such as Luther reach a mass audience before authorities could react. Rulers and the Church answered with licensing, censorship and the Index of Forbidden Books (1559), a contest over control of information that never ended.",
 now="Mass literacy, the newspaper and the idea of a reading public all grew from print. Today's fights over social media (disinformation, censorship, who controls distribution) replay arguments that began with the pamphlet wars of the 1520s.",
 lens="Ask who controls the means of spreading information. When it becomes cheap or changes hands, established authorities usually lose control of the story first and reassert it later."),
"af14410": dict(
 bluf="Portugal's raids and purchases on the West African coast began the Atlantic slave trade, which over four centuries forcibly moved about 12.5 million Africans. It built the plantation economies of the Americas, enriched European merchants and states, and drained and destabilized much of Atlantic Africa.",
 why="Portugal was a small, poor kingdom looking for gold and a sea route around Muslim-controlled trade. Slavery already existed in Africa, the Mediterranean and the Islamic world, so Portuguese captains could tap existing markets; within a few years they were buying captives from African rulers rather than raiding.",
 trig="In 1441 an expedition sent by Prince Henry seized Africans on the coast of today's Mauritania; a public sale of captives at Lagos, Portugal, followed in 1444.",
 then="Portugal shipped enslaved Africans to sugar islands such as Madeira and São Tomé, creating the plantation model (one export crop, worked by enslaved labor) later carried to Brazil and the Caribbean. Demand for captives fed warfare between African states that sold prisoners for guns, cloth and metal.",
 now="The trade shaped the population of the Americas (Brazil has the largest population of African descent outside Africa), created the racial hierarchies slavery required, and underlies today's debates over reparations and Africa's long-run development.",
 lens="Forced-labor systems grow around a profitable commodity. Follow the crop or the mineral and you will find who was coerced to produce it, and who profited."),
"as14430": dict(
 bluf="Hangul gave Koreans a phonetic alphabet designed for their own language instead of borrowed Chinese characters. The scholar elite resisted it for four centuries, but it became the foundation of modern Korean identity and near-universal literacy.",
 story="Korea's written language was classical Chinese, which took years to master and did not fit spoken Korean. King Sejong and a group of scholars designed Hangul, completed in 1443 and proclaimed in 1446 as the Hunminjeongeum, 'the correct sounds for instructing the people'. Its letter shapes are based on how the mouth and tongue form each sound.",
 why="Sejong wrote that ordinary people could not express their thoughts in writing because Chinese characters were too hard to learn. A script that fit Korean speech could spread literacy beyond the aristocracy.",
 then="Confucian scholar-officials, whose status rested on mastering Chinese, opposed it, and it survived mainly among women, commoners and in popular literature. It became the official script only in 1894; Japanese colonial rule (1910-45) suppressed Korean, which turned Hangul into a symbol of national resistance.",
 now="Both Koreas write almost entirely in Hangul and celebrate it with national holidays. It anchors a Korean identity distinct from China and Japan.",
 lens="Scripts and languages are political: who can read, and in which language, decides who can take part in power."),
"as14491": dict(
 bluf="The capture of the Ming emperor by Oirat Mongols ended Ming confidence in offensive war on the steppe. China switched to a defensive strategy built on walls and fortified garrisons, a posture that shaped its northern frontier for two centuries.",
 why="Steppe peoples wanted regular access to Chinese markets; when the Ming restricted the tribute trade, raids followed. At court, the eunuch Wang Zhen pushed the inexperienced emperor to lead the army in person.",
 trig="An Oirat invasion in 1449, after a quarrel over the size of their tribute-trade missions.",
 then="The court enthroned the emperor's brother, and the minister Yu Qian saved Beijing. The released emperor later took the throne back in a palace coup (1457). The Ming gave up campaigns into Mongolia and in the 1500s rebuilt the Great Wall in brick and stone.",
 now="Most of the Great Wall seen today is Ming-era. The episode is a classic example of how one defeat can make a state strategically defensive for generations.",
 lens="Major defeats often change a state's strategy more than victories do. Look for the trauma behind a country's defensive habits."),
"eu14531": dict(
 bluf="Mehmed II's conquest ended the last Roman state and gave the Ottomans an imperial capital straddling Europe and Asia. It made them the dominant power of the eastern Mediterranean for three centuries and showed that gunpowder artillery could break the strongest medieval fortifications.",
 why="Byzantium had been reduced to little more than the city itself, weakened by the Fourth Crusade's sack in 1204 and a century of Ottoman expansion through Anatolia and the Balkans. The city sat between the Ottomans' European and Asian lands and controlled the straits.",
 trig="Mehmed built a fortress on the Bosporus in 1452 to cut the city off, then besieged it from April 1453 with heavy cannon and a fleet.",
 then="Istanbul became the Ottoman capital, and the sultans claimed the Roman imperial inheritance. Expansion into the Balkans, the Arab lands and the Mediterranean followed. The Orthodox Church survived under Ottoman rule, while Moscow later styled itself the 'Third Rome'. The common claim that the fall 'closed' the spice routes and forced Europeans to sail is overstated: Portugal was already exploring Africa, and the Ottomans profited from trade rather than blocking it.",
 now="Turkey still controls the straits between the Black Sea and the Mediterranean, a lever in every Russia-West crisis. Hagia Sophia, reconverted to a mosque in 2020, remains a symbol in Turkish politics and Greek-Turkish relations.",
 lens="Watch the chokepoints: whoever holds a strait or a crossing between regions gains leverage far beyond their size."),
"eu14532": dict(
 who="Kings of England and France; Joan of Arc (1429-31)",
 bluf="England's defeat ended its centuries-old ambition to rule in France and fixed the two as separate kingdoms. The war forged national identities in both countries and gave the French crown a standing army and regular taxes.",
 why="English kings held lands in France as vassals of the French king, and from 1337 Edward III claimed the French crown itself through his mother. Disputes over Gascony, the Flemish wool trade and France's alliance with Scotland kept the conflict going for over a century.",
 trig="French victories at Formigny (1450) and Castillon (1453) drove the English out of Normandy and Gascony.",
 then="France came out with a permanent royal army and taxes the king could raise without an assembly's consent, a base for later absolutism. England, left only with Calais, fell into the Wars of the Roses and over time turned toward the sea.",
 now="The war gave both countries founding myths (Joan of Arc in France, Agincourt in England) and a habit of seeing each other as the defining rival.",
 lens="Long wars build states: armies and taxes created to fight them rarely disappear when the fighting stops."),
"d-gutbible": dict(
 bluf="The Gutenberg Bible proved that print could match the quality of the finest manuscripts. That convinced buyers and investors that printing was a viable business, which is why presses spread across Europe within a generation.",
 why="Gutenberg needed a prestigious, high-value product to repay heavy loans, and the Bible had a guaranteed market among churches and monasteries.",
 then="His backer Johann Fust sued in 1455 and took over much of the press. Trained printers carried the technique to other cities; by 1500 presses were running in over 250 towns.",
 now="Fewer than 50 copies survive; they mark the start of European printing.",
 lens="New technologies spread once they first match the old standard in quality and then beat it on cost."),
"eu14553": dict(
 bluf="Three decades of civil war between rival branches of the royal family weakened England's great noble houses. The eventual winner, Henry Tudor, built a stronger, more centralized monarchy.",
 why="Defeat in France discredited Henry VI's government and brought home nobles with armed retinues. A weak king, at times mentally incapacitated, could not control rival magnates competing for power at court.",
 trig="Richard, Duke of York, took up arms against the king's favourites at St Albans in May 1455.",
 then="Many noble lines died out or were ruined. Henry VII, after 1485, curbed private armies, rebuilt royal finances and ruled through loyal officials, laying the base of the Tudor state.",
 now="The Tudor dynasty that emerged broke with Rome and began England's rise as a naval power. Shakespeare's history plays fixed the popular memory of the wars.",
 lens="Losing a foreign war often brings a reckoning at home: discredited rulers and returning armed men are a recipe for civil conflict."),
"d-trebizond": dict(
 bluf="Trebizond's fall eliminated the last Greek successor state of Byzantium and completed Mehmed II's absorption of the Byzantine world. It also gave the Ottomans the southern coast of the Black Sea.",
 why="Mehmed set out to remove every rival claimant to the Roman inheritance and to secure Black Sea trade. Trebizond had sought an alliance against him with Uzun Hasan, ruler of a rival Turkmen state.",
 then="Within two decades the Black Sea became largely an Ottoman lake; the Crimean Khanate became a vassal in 1475. Greek Orthodox communities remained on the Pontic coast until the expulsions and population exchange of the 1920s.",
 now="Trabzon is a major Turkish port, and the lost Pontic homeland is still part of Greek diaspora memory.",
 lens="Empires consolidate by eliminating rival claimants to legitimacy, not just rival armies."),
"af14641": dict(
 bluf="Sonni Ali turned Songhai from a regional kingdom into West Africa's largest empire, controlling the Niger River cities that dominated the trans-Saharan trade in gold and salt. Songhai's rise shows that West African power was built on long-distance commerce and Islamic learning.",
 story="Sonni Ali led cavalry and a war fleet on the Niger to seize Timbuktu (1468) and, after a long siege, Djenné, the trading and learning cities of the fading Mali Empire. By his death in 1492 Songhai controlled the great bend of the Niger.",
 why="Mali was weakened by succession disputes and by attacks from the Tuareg and the Mossi. Whoever held the Niger bend controlled the exchange of Saharan salt for gold from the forest zone to the south.",
 then="Songhai dominated the region for a century, until a Moroccan army with firearms defeated it at Tondibi in 1591; the region then fragmented into smaller states.",
 now="Mali and Niger claim Songhai as part of their national heritage. The same river and desert corridors are now fought over by jihadist groups, smugglers and military governments.",
 lens="In the Sahel, power has long followed control of trade corridors. The same routes matter for today's smuggling networks and insurgencies."),
"as14673": dict(
 bluf="A succession dispute in the shogun's court destroyed Kyoto and the shogunate's real authority, opening Japan's century of 'warring states'. Power passed to regional warlords, the daimyo, whose competition eventually produced unification under the Tokugawa.",
 why="The Ashikaga shoguns depended on powerful provincial governors they could not control. When the shogun Yoshimasa's succession was disputed, the Hosokawa and Yamana factions backed rival candidates.",
 trig="Fighting broke out in Kyoto in 1467 over the shogunal succession and a feud within the Hatakeyama family.",
 then="Central authority collapsed. Lords built domains with their own laws and castles, adopted firearms after 1543, and fought one another until Oda Nobunaga, Toyotomi Hideyoshi and Tokugawa Ieyasu reunified the country (1568-1603).",
 now="The warrior culture of this era shaped Japan's feudal order and explains the rigid central control the Tokugawa imposed to prevent another collapse.",
 lens="When a central government depends on regional strongmen it cannot discipline, one succession crisis can trigger generations of fragmentation."),
"eu14694": dict(
 bluf="The marriage united Castile and Aragon under one couple, the core of the future Spanish state. Their joint rule produced the conquest of Granada, the Inquisition, the expulsion of the Jews and Columbus's voyages: a programme of religious unity and expansion that defined early modern Spain.",
 why="Isabella needed allies to win a disputed succession in Castile. Aragon, a Mediterranean power, wanted Castile's larger population and resources against France.",
 then="The two crowns kept their own laws and institutions but acted together in war, foreign policy and religion. Their children married into the Habsburgs, so their grandson Charles V inherited a world empire.",
 now="Spain's tension between the central state and regions with their own identities, such as Catalonia (once part of the Crown of Aragon), goes back to this union of separate kingdoms.",
 lens="Many European states were assembled by dynastic marriage. The seams between the joined parts often still show in regional politics."),
"eu14785": dict(
 bluf="The Inquisition made religious conformity a matter of state power in Spain. Aimed first at converted Jews and later at Muslim converts and Protestants, it tied Spanish identity to Catholic orthodoxy and 'purity of blood' for three centuries.",
 why="Forced and mass conversions of Jews after the riots of 1391 created a large group of 'New Christians' who rose in trade and government, provoking resentment and suspicion that they secretly practised Judaism. The monarchs wanted a royal-controlled institution to enforce unity across their newly joined kingdoms.",
 then="It tried tens of thousands of people and executed a few thousand, most in its first decades. It drove the expulsions of 1492 and 1609 and enforced 'purity of blood' rules that barred people of Jewish or Muslim descent from many offices. It was abolished only in 1834.",
 now="Its premise that ancestry could make a person's loyalty suspect foreshadowed later racial thinking, and it remains the standard reference for state-backed religious persecution.",
 lens="States consolidating power often define the nation by excluding an internal 'other'. Watch for laws that make loyalty a matter of descent."),
"d-otranto": dict(
 bluf="The seizure of Otranto was the high-water mark of Ottoman ambitions in Italy. Mehmed II's death ended the campaign within a year, but the scare shaped papal and Italian politics for decades.",
 why="Mehmed saw himself as heir to Rome and had conquered the Balkans; Italy's divided states, constantly at war with each other, looked vulnerable.",
 then="Neapolitan forces retook the town in 1481. Later sultans pursued the Mediterranean through naval power and North African allies rather than invasion of Italy.",
 now="The 'Martyrs of Otranto', canonized in 2013, still feature in European debates over Christian-Muslim history.",
 lens="In personalized regimes, a ruler's death can reverse a strategy overnight. Ask how much a policy depends on one person."),
"af14822": dict(
 bluf="Elmina was the first permanent European fort in sub-Saharan Africa, built to capture the Gold Coast's gold trade. It became the model for the chain of coastal forts through which Europeans traded with, and later shipped captives from, West Africa.",
 story="In 1482 King João II sent Diogo de Azambuja with pre-cut stone to build São Jorge da Mina on the Gold Coast, after negotiating with a reluctant local Akan ruler. The fort gave Portugal a secure base to buy gold directly at the coast. Later it held enslaved Africans awaiting ships to the Americas.",
 why="Gold from the Akan forest had traditionally travelled north across the Sahara. Portugal wanted to divert it to the sea and keep rivals, especially Castile, away.",
 then="Gold flowed to Lisbon and financed Portugal's expansion. The Dutch captured Elmina in 1637 and expanded its role in the slave trade; dozens of European forts followed along the coast.",
 now="Elmina and Cape Coast Castle, both in Ghana, are central sites of memory for the slave trade and for African diaspora visitors.",
 lens="Coastal forts mark the shift from Europeans as traders on African terms to Europeans as armed competitors on African soil."),
"eu14856": dict(
 bluf="Henry Tudor's victory at Bosworth ended the Wars of the Roses and founded the dynasty that built England's centralized state. The Tudors broke with Rome, built a navy and laid the political groundwork for England's later expansion.",
 story="At Bosworth Field in August 1485, Richard III was abandoned by key allies, notably the Stanley family, and killed in the fighting. Henry VII married Elizabeth of York, uniting the rival houses of Lancaster and York.",
 why="Richard III's seizure of the throne in 1483, and the disappearance of his young nephews, turned many nobles against him. Henry, the leading Lancastrian claimant, raised an army in exile with French support.",
 then="Henry VII restored royal finances, curbed nobles' private armies and ruled through councils of professional officials. His son Henry VIII and granddaughter Elizabeth I took this stronger state into the Reformation and a naval rivalry with Spain.",
 now="England's national church, its navy and much of its national story, as told by Shakespeare, come from the Tudor century.",
 lens="New dynasties that win power by force usually spend their first years making sure no one can overthrow them the same way."),
"af14883": dict(
 bluf="Dias proved that the Atlantic and Indian Oceans connect, opening a sea route from Europe to Asia that bypassed Mediterranean and Middle Eastern middlemen. Ten years later Vasco da Gama used it to reach India.",
 why="Portugal had spent decades working down the African coast, funded by gold and slaves, looking for a way into the spice trade.",
 then="The route made Portugal an Indian Ocean power within twenty years. The Cape later became a supply station for Dutch and British shipping and the base of white settlement in South Africa.",
 now="The Cape route still matters: when the Suez Canal or the Red Sea is blocked, as during the Houthi attacks on shipping from 2023, ships reroute around it.",
 lens="Geography rules until technology changes it: new routes reshuffle which places are central and which are bypassed."),
"la14920": dict(
 bluf="Columbus's landing began permanent contact between the Americas and the rest of the world, the largest exchange of people, plants, animals and diseases in history. It led to the conquest and depopulation of Indigenous societies and to European empires that reshaped the global economy.",
 why="Iberian kingdoms were competing for a route to Asian trade. Columbus badly underestimated the size of the earth, and Castile, freed by its conquest of Granada, gambled on his plan.",
 trig="Ferdinand and Isabella agreed in April 1492 to fund the voyage; Columbus landed in the Bahamas on October 12.",
 then="Spanish settlement, forced labor and epidemics destroyed the Taíno within decades. The Columbian Exchange carried maize and potatoes to Europe, Africa and Asia, fuelling population growth, while horses, cattle, wheat and smallpox went west. American silver later financed Spain and flowed on to China.",
 now="The populations, languages, religions and racial hierarchies of the Americas all derive from this encounter. The fight over Columbus Day versus Indigenous Peoples' Day shows how contested its meaning remains.",
 lens="Biology can decide history: disease did more than guns to determine who would hold the Americas."),
"eu14927": dict(
 bluf="Granada's surrender completed the Christian conquest of Iberia and freed Castile's resources for overseas ventures. It also began a policy of enforced religious unity that soon turned against Spain's Muslims and Jews.",
 why="The Nasrid emirate had survived for two centuries as a tributary of Castile, but feuds within its ruling family and the combined power of Castile and Aragon left it isolated.",
 then="The surrender terms promised Muslims freedom of worship, but forced conversions began within a decade (1499-1502), and the converted Moriscos were expelled in 1609-14. Three months after the surrender, the monarchs agreed to fund Columbus.",
 now="Andalusia's Islamic heritage, from the Alhambra to its place names, is part of Spanish identity. 'Al-Andalus' is remembered in the Arab world as a lost golden age and in Europe as a reference point in debates over coexistence.",
 lens="Promises of tolerance in surrender treaties tend to last only as long as the defeated keep some leverage."),
"eu14927b": dict(
 bluf="The expulsion ended centuries of Jewish life in Spain and created the Sephardic diaspora across the Mediterranean and Europe. It expressed a new principle of statehood: one realm, one faith.",
 why="The Inquisition argued that practising Jews were drawing converts back to Judaism. After taking Granada, the monarchs made religious uniformity the basis of their joint rule.",
 then="Tens of thousands left for Portugal (which forced its Jews to convert in 1497), North Africa, Italy and the Ottoman Empire, where Sultan Bayezid II welcomed them. Sephardic merchants later built trade networks in Amsterdam, London and the Americas. Spain lost artisans, financiers and physicians.",
 now="Sephardic culture and the Ladino language survive in Israel, Turkey and the Americas. Spain's and Portugal's 2015 citizenship laws for descendants were a formal acknowledgment of the wrong.",
 lens="Expelling a minority often hands its skills and networks to rival states."),
"af14934": dict(
 bluf="Askia Muhammad turned Songhai into a centralized Islamic state with a professional army and provincial governors. Timbuktu's scholarly fame under his rule shows how fully West Africa was part of the wider Muslim world.",
 why="After Sonni Ali died, his general Muhammad Ture overthrew Sonni Ali's son, drawing support from Muslim merchants and scholars who had been uneasy with Sonni Ali's rule.",
 trig="He defeated Sonni Ali's son in 1493 and took the title Askia.",
 then="He made the pilgrimage to Mecca in 1496-97, returned with recognition as caliph of the western Sudan, and expanded the empire. Succession struggles after his reign weakened Songhai, which fell to Morocco in 1591.",
 now="The Timbuktu manuscripts, on law, astronomy and medicine, refute the idea of a pre-colonial Africa without written history. Jihadists tried to burn them in 2013.",
 lens="Rulers who take power by force often seek religious legitimacy. Watch whom a new ruler courts."),
"d-aldine": dict(
 bluf="Aldus Manutius turned printing from copying old manuscripts into publishing: carefully edited Greek and Latin texts in portable, affordable formats. His press made classical learning a shared European resource and set design standards books still use.",
 why="Venice was Europe's printing capital, rich in capital and trade routes, and Greek scholars fleeing the Ottomans had brought manuscripts and expertise.",
 then="Aldine editions of Aristotle, Plato and others spread humanist scholarship. The small-format book let people read privately, away from libraries and lecterns.",
 now="Italic type and the modern semicolon come from his press; the paperback is a distant descendant.",
 lens="Format matters: making knowledge portable and affordable changes who uses it."),
"eu14948": dict(
 bluf="France's invasion turned Italy into the battlefield of Europe's great powers for 65 years and ended the independence of most Italian states. The wars produced the European state system: balance of power, permanent embassies and shifting alliances.",
 why="France and Spain, newly consolidated monarchies, had dynastic claims to Naples and Milan. Italy's wealthy but divided city-states invited intervention, and sometimes invited it in themselves.",
 trig="Milan's ruler Ludovico Sforza encouraged Charles VIII of France to press his claim to Naples in 1494.",
 then="Spain won control of Naples and Milan by the Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis (1559). Italian states learned that only alliances could hold back larger powers, which gave rise to resident ambassadors and balance-of-power diplomacy. New artillery and fortress designs spread across Europe.",
 now="Italy remained divided and largely under foreign influence until unification in the 1860s. The balance-of-power logic first practised here still underlies thinking about international relations.",
 lens="Rich but divided regions attract intervention by larger neighbours, and internal rivals often invite the outsider in."),
"la14941": dict(
 bluf="Spain and Portugal divided the newly reachable world between them by treaty, without consulting anyone who lived there. The line explains why Brazil speaks Portuguese and set the precedent of European powers carving up other continents on maps.",
 why="Columbus's return set off a dispute over who owned newly found lands. Pope Alexander VI issued decrees favouring Spain, and Portugal negotiated directly to move the line west.",
 then="Brazil's eastern coast fell on Portugal's side. France, England and the Dutch rejected the treaty and argued that land belonged to whoever actually occupied it.",
 now="Beyond Brazil's language and culture, the idea that distant powers could draw colonial borders returned at the Berlin Conference of 1884-85.",
 lens="Borders drawn by outsiders on maps tend to outlast the empires that drew them."),
"na14970": dict(
 bluf="Cabot's voyage gave England its legal claim to North America, the basis for later colonies. The cod fishery off Newfoundland drew European fishermen for centuries and pulled the North Atlantic into the European economy.",
 why="After Columbus, Henry VII wanted a share of the western routes, and Bristol merchants already fished far out into the Atlantic.",
 then="England did little for a century, but revived the claim to justify Newfoundland (1583) and Jamestown (1607). Basque, Portuguese, French and English fleets worked the cod banks, and salt cod became a major traded staple.",
 now="Canada's Atlantic provinces grew from the fishery. Its collapse through overfishing led to Canada's cod moratorium in 1992, a lesson in exhausting a common resource.",
 lens="Legal claims are often made long before a state has the power to use them, then revived when it does."),
"as14984": dict(
 bluf="Da Gama's voyage created the first direct sea link between Europe and Asia and began European armed intervention in Indian Ocean trade. It was the start of European empire in Asia.",
 story="Vasco da Gama sailed from Lisbon in July 1497, rounded the Cape and, with a pilot hired in Malindi, crossed the Indian Ocean to Calicut in May 1498. The ruler of Calicut was unimpressed by his gifts, but the pepper and spices he brought home were worth many times the cost of the voyage.",
 why="Portugal wanted direct access to Asian spices, which reached Europe through Muslim and Venetian middlemen at high markups. Decades of African voyages had built the necessary navigational knowledge.",
 then="Portugal used naval guns to impose licensed trade, seizing Goa, Malacca and Hormuz. Indian Ocean commerce had been open to many merchant communities, with no single power controlling the sea; Portugal tried to tax and control it by force, a model the Dutch and English later expanded.",
 now="The sea route was the foundation of European colonialism in Asia. The Indian Ocean is again a zone of naval competition, now among India, China and the United States.",
 lens="Control of sea lanes turns naval power into economic power. Watch who patrols the trade routes."),
"la15002": dict(
 bluf="Cabral's landing gave Portugal Brazil, which became the largest and richest part of its empire. Brazil grew on sugar, gold and enslaved African labor and became the largest slave society in the Americas.",
 why="Sailing far west to catch the Atlantic winds that carried ships around Africa, his fleet reached South America inside the zone Tordesillas had given Portugal.",
 then="Portugal first exploited brazilwood, then built sugar plantations in the northeast worked by enslaved Africans; gold strikes in the 1690s drew settlers inland. Brazil received about 40% of all Africans shipped across the Atlantic.",
 now="Brazil is the largest Portuguese-speaking nation and has the largest population of African descent outside Africa. Its racial inequalities trace back to the plantation economy.",
 lens="Whether a place becomes a colony often begins with accident; what happens next depends on what can be extracted there."),
"as15015": dict(
 bluf="Shah Ismail founded the Safavid state and imposed Twelver Shi'ism on a mostly Sunni Iran. That gave Iran a religious identity distinct from its Ottoman and Central Asian neighbours, a divide that still shapes Middle Eastern politics.",
 story="Ismail, hereditary head of the Safavid Sufi order and backed by Turkmen warriors called the Qizilbash, took Tabriz in 1501 at about 14 and crowned himself shah. He declared Twelver Shi'a Islam the state religion, enforced it at times violently, and brought in Shi'a scholars from Lebanon and Bahrain to build a clerical establishment.",
 why="The collapse of earlier Turkmen states left a power vacuum. Ismail's movement combined charismatic religious authority with tribal military strength, and Shi'ism set the new state apart from the Sunni Ottomans and Uzbeks.",
 then="Iran became the heartland of Shi'a Islam, with a clerical class that later gained independent power. The Ottoman-Safavid wars fixed a frontier through Mesopotamia and the Caucasus.",
 now="Iran remains the leading Shi'a state and backs Shi'a and allied groups across the region; the Saudi-Iranian rivalry is often framed in Sunni-Shi'a terms.",
 lens="Religion can be a tool of state-building: a distinct faith gives a new state borders of identity, not just of territory."),
"eu15039": dict(
 bluf="Leonardo's portrait of a merchant's wife shows the Renaissance shift in art toward secular subjects, individual personality and techniques grounded in close study of nature. Its later fame, built by royal collecting, a famous theft and mass tourism, shows how cultural icons are made.",
 why="Wealthy merchant families in Florence commissioned portraits to display status. Leonardo's study of anatomy and optics let him paint soft, lifelike transitions of light (sfumato).",
 then="Its composition, a half-length figure turned three-quarters against a landscape, became a model for European portraiture. It passed into the French royal collection and, after the Revolution, into the Louvre, a public museum.",
 now="Its global celebrity dates from its theft in 1911. It is a case study in how states use art as soft power and how tourism shapes heritage.",
 lens="Art follows money: when merchants become patrons, art turns to portraits of merchants."),
"af15055": dict(
 bluf="The sack of Kilwa marked Portugal's violent entry into the trading world of the Swahili coast. Portuguese attempts to control trade by force weakened city-states that had prospered for centuries as links between Africa and the Indian Ocean.",
 story="Kilwa Kisiwani, an island city in today's Tanzania, had grown rich on gold from the Zimbabwe plateau shipped through Sofala, and had minted its own coins. In 1505 Francisco de Almeida's fleet stormed and plundered it and installed a ruler loyal to Portugal.",
 why="Portugal wanted the gold of Sofala and bases on the route to India, and tried to force Muslim merchant cities to pay tribute and buy trade licences.",
 then="Kilwa declined, and Mombasa was sacked the same year. Portugal held Fort Jesus at Mombasa until Omani Arabs drove it out in 1698; Oman, and later Zanzibar, then dominated the coast.",
 now="Swahili, an African language with Arabic and Persian loanwords, ties East Africa to the Indian Ocean world. Kilwa's ruins are a UNESCO site.",
 lens="Trading cities that live by openness are vulnerable to any power that brings guns to the market."),
"eu150810": dict(
 bluf="Michelangelo's ceiling shows the Renaissance papacy acting as a princely power: patron of art, builder of Rome and player in Italian wars. The same lavish spending, especially on the new St Peter's, was financed partly by indulgence sales that soon provoked Luther.",
 why="Julius II, the 'warrior pope', aimed to restore papal authority and glorify Rome, and he commissioned Michelangelo to decorate the papal chapel.",
 then="The ceiling (1508-12) raised artists' status from craftsmen to geniuses and set a standard for monumental art. Within five years, anger at papal finances erupted in Germany.",
 now="Cardinals still elect popes beneath it, and the Vatican's cultural prestige remains a form of soft power.",
 lens="Splendour can signal strength or overreach. Ask who is paying for a regime's monuments, and how."),
"as15106": dict(
 bluf="The capture of Goa gave Portugal a permanent base and capital in Asia, the hub of a network of fortified ports meant to control the spice trade. Goa stayed Portuguese for 451 years, one of the longest-lasting European colonies in Asia.",
 why="Albuquerque wanted naval bases rather than large territories. Goa had a good harbour and controlled the lucrative trade in war horses to southern Indian kingdoms.",
 then="Goa became the seat of Portugal's viceroy, a centre of Catholic missions (with an Inquisition from 1560) and a mixed Indo-Portuguese society.",
 now="India annexed Goa in 1961 after Portugal refused to leave. Its Catholic heritage and distinct culture still set it apart within India.",
 lens="Early European empires in Asia were networks of ports, not territories: control the nodes and tax the trade."),
"as15117": dict(
 bluf="Taking Malacca gave Portugal the chokepoint between the Indian Ocean and the South China Sea. Control of the strait passed from Portugal to the Dutch to the British, and its strategic importance has never faded.",
 why="Malacca was the main port where Indian, Arab, Chinese and Malay merchants met; holding it meant taxing the spice trade from the Moluccas.",
 then="Muslim merchants moved to rival ports such as Aceh, Johor and Banten, spreading trade and Islam across the archipelago, and Portugal never controlled the region's commerce as it hoped. The Dutch took Malacca in 1641 and the British in 1824.",
 now="The Strait of Malacca carries a large share of world trade and most of China's oil imports, a vulnerability Chinese strategists call the 'Malacca dilemma'.",
 lens="Chokepoints concentrate leverage. The states that controlled them in the past, and those that do now, shape regional power."),
"eu151311": dict(
 bluf="The Prince separated politics from Christian morality, analysing power as it is actually practised rather than as it ought to be. It founded the 'realist' tradition in political thought.",
 why="Machiavelli had served the Florentine republic as a diplomat during the Italian Wars and watched foreign armies overrun Italy. Dismissed when the Medici returned in 1512, he wrote to show how a prince could seize, hold and defend a state.",
 then="The Church put it on the Index of Forbidden Books in 1559, but rulers and political writers across Europe read it. His Discourses on Livy, praising republics, influenced later republican thought.",
 now="Realism in international relations, the view that states pursue power and security rather than morality, descends from his approach.",
 lens="Judge political actors by their incentives and constraints, not by their stated principles."),
"la15133": dict(
 bluf="Balboa's crossing showed that a narrow isthmus separated the Atlantic from a vast ocean, confirming that the Americas lay between Europe and Asia. Panama became Spain's crossroads for moving Peruvian silver and, four centuries later, the site of a canal.",
 why="Indigenous people told Spanish settlers on the Caribbean coast of a sea and of rich lands to the south.",
 then="Magellan's expedition crossed the Pacific in 1520-21, and Pizarro launched the conquest of the Inca from Panama. Peruvian silver crossed the isthmus on its way to Spain.",
 now="The Panama Canal (1914) uses the same narrow crossing and remains a strategic chokepoint that both the United States and China watch closely.",
 lens="Geography that makes a place a crossroads also makes it a target for outside control."),
"me-chaldiran": dict(
 bluf="Chaldiran stopped Safavid expansion into Anatolia and fixed the frontier between the Sunni Ottoman and Shi'a Safavid worlds. It also showed the power of gunpowder armies over traditional cavalry.",
 why="Shah Ismail's Shi'a missionaries were winning followers among Anatolia's Turkmen tribes, threatening Ottoman control of the east; Selim I repressed suspected Safavid sympathizers before marching.",
 then="Selim took eastern Anatolia, then turned south and conquered the Mamluks (1516-17). The Safavids adopted firearms and later moved their capital away from the exposed frontier.",
 now="Turkey's border with Iran broadly follows the line fixed by the Treaty of Zuhab (1639), which ended the wars this battle began. The Sunni-Shi'a divide between the two states persists.",
 lens="Military revolutions reward states that can raise money and organize supply for gunpowder armies over states built on tribal cavalry."),
"af15176": dict(
 bluf="Selim I's conquest of the Mamluk sultanate doubled Ottoman territory and gave the sultans Cairo, Damascus and guardianship of Mecca and Medina. The Ottomans became the leading Sunni Muslim power and ruled the Arab heartlands for 400 years.",
 why="The two empires competed for the frontier lands of southeastern Anatolia and Syria. The Mamluks were weakened by Portuguese disruption of Red Sea trade and had been slow to adopt firearms.",
 trig="Fearing a Mamluk-Safavid alliance after Chaldiran, Selim marched south in 1516.",
 then="The sultans took the title 'Servant of the Two Holy Sanctuaries' and later claimed the caliphate. Egypt's grain and taxes funded the empire, while Mamluk households kept real power in Egyptian politics under Ottoman governors.",
 now="Arab memory of the 'Ottoman centuries', seen by some as foreign rule and by others as Islamic unity, starts here. Custody of the holy cities still confers legitimacy, as the Saudi king's title 'Custodian of the Two Holy Mosques' shows.",
 lens="Control of religious sites gives rulers legitimacy that armies alone cannot."),
"eu151712": dict(
 bluf="Luther's challenge broke the Catholic Church's religious monopoly in Western Europe. By resting authority on scripture and individual conscience instead of the Pope, and spreading his case through print, he set off a century of religious war and shifted power from Rome to princes and states.",
 why="Germans resented papal taxation and clerical corruption. The Holy Roman Empire was politically fragmented, so princes could protect a dissident; humanists were already urging a return to original texts; and printing could spread a message faster than authorities could suppress it.",
 trig="Johann Tetzel's sale of indulgences to fund the rebuilding of St Peter's in Rome provoked Luther's 95 Theses in October 1517.",
 then="Princes and cities that adopted Protestantism seized church lands and took control of religion in their territories, a principle formalized in 1555 as 'whose realm, his religion'. Western Christianity split into Lutheran, Reformed and Anglican churches; the Catholic Counter-Reformation answered. Religious wars lasted until 1648.",
 now="Protestantism shaped literacy, state churches and the religious map of Europe and North America. Evangelical Protestantism is now among the fastest-growing faiths in Africa and Latin America.",
 lens="Ideas spread when they serve political interests: Luther survived because powerful princes found his message useful."),
"eu151913": dict(
 bluf="Charles V's inheritance put Spain, the Low Countries, Austria, southern Italy and the Americas under one man. His empire was too large and scattered to govern as a whole, and its wars with France, the Ottomans and the Protestants defined the 16th century.",
 why="A chain of dynastic marriages and early deaths gave Charles the inheritances of Castile, Aragon, Burgundy and Austria. He then won election as emperor by paying the German electors, with loans from the Fugger banking family.",
 then="He fought France across Italy, defended Vienna against the Ottomans (1529) and failed to stop the Reformation. In 1556 he abdicated and split his lands between Spanish and Austrian branches of the Habsburgs. American silver paid for his wars but left Spain heavily in debt.",
 now="The Habsburgs ruled Austria until 1918, and the Spanish empire's legacy spans Latin America. The medieval ideal of one ruler for all of Christendom died with his failure.",
 lens="Empires assembled by inheritance rather than conquest often lack a common identity or interest to hold them together."),
"la15194": dict(
 bluf="Cortés's landing began the conquest of the Aztec Empire, the most powerful state in Mesoamerica. The outcome depended less on Spanish weapons than on alliances with Indigenous peoples who resented Aztec rule, and on smallpox.",
 story="Hernán Cortés landed on the Gulf coast in 1519 with about 500 men, defying his governor in Cuba, and scuttled his ships so his men could not turn back. With Malinche, a Nahua woman who became his interpreter and adviser, he allied with the Totonacs and then with Tlaxcala, longtime enemies of the Aztecs, and marched on Tenochtitlan.",
 why="Cortés wanted gold, land and glory, and he exploited the hatred that subject and rival peoples felt for Aztec tribute demands and human sacrifice.",
 then="Tenochtitlan fell in 1521 after a siege fought largely by tens of thousands of Indigenous allies, during a smallpox epidemic. Spain built New Spain on top of Aztec systems of tribute and labor.",
 now="Mexico's mestizo identity, and the long argument over Malinche (traitor or survivor), show how this encounter still shapes how Mexicans see themselves.",
 lens="Small outside forces topple large empires mostly by mobilizing the empire's own discontented subjects."),
}
